{"timestamp": "2025-08-02T02:32:48.751651", "function_name": "collect", "function_body": "  state = env._current_state\n  cookbook = env.world.cookbook\n  \n  # 1. Map the primitive name to its integer index using the cookbook's index.\n  # If the primitive name is not recognized, it cannot be collected.\n  try:\n    primitive_idx = cookbook.index[primitive]\n  except KeyError:\n    return [] # Invalid primitive name, no actions can be generated.\n\n  # 2. Verify if the target primitive is actually a grabbable item.\n  # Items like workshops or environment elements (water, boundaries) are not grabbable.\n  # Attempting to 'collect' a non-grabbable item via 'USE' will not add it to inventory.\n  if primitive_idx not in env.world.grabbable_indices:\n      return [] # Cannot collect this type of item.\n\n  # 3. Check if the primitive is already in the agent's inventory.\n  # If the agent already possesses the item, no further collection actions are needed.\n  if state.inventory[primitive_idx] > 0:\n    return [] # Item already collected.\n\n  # --- Extract relevant environment and agent state information ---\n  grid = state.grid\n  agent_pos = state.pos  # Current (x, y) position of the agent.\n  grid_width, grid_height, _ = grid.shape # Dimensions of the game grid.\n\n  # Retrieve integer mappings for standard movement actions and the 'USE' action.\n  action_map = env.action_specs()\n  UP = action_map['UP']\n  DOWN = action_map['DOWN']\n  LEFT = action_map['LEFT']\n  RIGHT = action_map['RIGHT']\n  USE = action_map['USE']\n\n  # Define a mapping from relative movement vectors (dx, dy) to their corresponding action indices.\n  # This is used for reconstructing the path from the BFS parent pointers.\n  dir_to_action = {\n      (0, -1): UP,    # Moving up means decreasing y-coordinate.\n      (0, 1): DOWN,   # Moving down means increasing y-coordinate.\n      (-1, 0): LEFT,  # Moving left means decreasing x-coordinate.\n      (1, 0): RIGHT,  # Moving right means increasing x-coordinate.\n  }\n\n  # 4. Identify impassable terrain types from the cookbook's index.\n  # These are entities that the agent cannot move through.\n  impassable_indices = set()\n  # 'BOUNDARY' and 'WATER' are typical impassable elements in CraftWorld.\n  if 'BOUNDARY' in cookbook.index.contents:\n      impassable_indices.add(cookbook.index['BOUNDARY'])\n  if 'WATER' in cookbook.index.contents:\n      impassable_indices.add(cookbook.index['WATER'])\n\n  # Helper function to check if a given grid cell is traversable.\n  # A cell is traversable if it's within grid boundaries and doesn't contain any impassable objects.\n  def is_cell_traversable(x, y):\n      # Check if coordinates are within the grid bounds.\n      if not (0 <= x < grid_width and 0 <= y < grid_height):\n          return False  # Outside grid boundaries.\n      \n      # Check if the cell contains any object from the `impassable_indices` set.\n      # The grid is assumed to be one-hot encoded along its last dimension for entity presence.\n      for imp_idx in impassable_indices:\n          if grid[x, y, imp_idx] == 1:\n              return False  # Cell contains an impassable object.\n      return True # Cell is traversable.\n\n  # 5. Pre-check: Ensure the agent's current starting position is traversable.\n  # If the agent starts in an impassable cell, no movement is possible.\n  if not is_cell_traversable(agent_pos[0], agent_pos[1]):\n      return [] # Agent starts in an invalid/impassable position.\n\n  # 6. Locate all instances of the primitive on the current grid.\n  # `np.argwhere` returns an array of (x, y) coordinates where the primitive is present.\n  primitive_locations = np.argwhere(grid[:, :, primitive_idx] == 1)\n\n  # If the primitive is not found anywhere on the grid, it cannot be collected.\n  if len(primitive_locations) == 0:\n    return [] # Primitive is not present on the grid.\n\n  # 7. Define the set of valid target cells for the BFS.\n  # These are any traversable cells from which the agent can successfully\n  # perform the 'USE' action to collect the primitive.\n  # The 'USE' action in CraftWorld typically affects the 3x3 neighborhood around the agent.\n  bfs_target_cells = set()\n  for px, py in primitive_locations: # For each location where the primitive exists:\n    # Examine all cells within the 3x3 neighborhood around the primitive.\n    for dx_n in [-1, 0, 1]:\n      for dy_n in [-1, 0, 1]:\n        nx, ny = px + dx_n, py + dy_n # Candidate cell for the agent to move to.\n        \n        # A valid target cell must be traversable by the agent.\n        if is_cell_traversable(nx, ny):\n          bfs_target_cells.add((nx, ny))\n\n  # If no traversable cells exist from which to interact with the primitive\n  # (e.g., the primitive is completely surrounded by impassable terrain), it's unreachable.\n  if not bfs_target_cells:\n      return [] # Primitive is unreachable due to surrounding impassable terrain.\n\n  # 8. Perform Breadth-First Search (BFS) to find the shortest path.\n  # The queue stores tuples: `(current_position, list_of_actions_taken_to_reach_here)`.\n  q = collections.deque([(agent_pos, [])])\n  # `visited` set keeps track of cells already added to the queue to prevent cycles and redundant paths.\n  visited = {agent_pos} \n  \n  while q:\n    current_pos, path_so_far = q.popleft()\n    \n    # If the current position is one of our valid BFS target cells, we've found\n    # the shortest path to a location from which the primitive can be 'USE'd.\n    if current_pos in bfs_target_cells:\n      # Append the 'USE' action to the path and return the complete sequence.\n      return path_so_far + [USE]\n\n    # Explore valid cardinal neighbors (UP, DOWN, LEFT, RIGHT) from the current position.\n    for dx, dy in [(0, -1), (0, 1), (-1, 0), (1, 0)]: # Possible relative movements.\n      next_x, next_y = current_pos[0] + dx, current_pos[1] + dy\n      next_pos = (next_x, next_y)\n\n      # Check if the next position is traversable and has not been visited yet (to find shortest path).\n      if is_cell_traversable(next_x, next_y) and next_pos not in visited:\n        visited.add(next_pos) # Mark as visited.\n        # Create a new path by appending the action required to move to `next_pos`.\n        new_path = path_so_far + [dir_to_action[(dx, dy)]]\n        q.append((next_pos, new_path)) # Add to queue for further exploration.\n  \n  # 9. If the BFS queue becomes empty and no path was found to any target cell,\n  # it means the primitive is unreachable from the current state.\n  return [] # Primitive cannot be reached or collected.", "island_id": 7, "scores": {"3": 0.5}}
{"timestamp": "2025-08-02T02:33:13.045857", "function_name": "collect", "function_body": "  try:\n    primitive_idx = env.world.cookbook.index.index(primitive)\n  except KeyError:\n    # If the primitive string is not known to the cookbook, it cannot be collected.\n    return []\n\n  # Check if the primitive is actually a grabbable item.\n  # The 'collect' function implies acquiring an item into inventory.\n  # If the primitive is marked as non-grabbable in the world configuration,\n  # it cannot be 'collected' in this manner, so no actions are returned.\n  if primitive_idx in env.world.non_grabbable_indices:\n    return []\n\n  # Get current state details\n  current_state = env._current_state\n  grid = current_state.grid\n  start_pos = current_state.pos\n  grid_width, grid_height, _ = grid.shape\n\n  # Get action mappings from the environment\n  action_map = env.action_specs()\n  ACTION_UP = action_map['UP']\n  ACTION_DOWN = action_map['DOWN']\n  ACTION_LEFT = action_map['LEFT']\n  ACTION_RIGHT = action_map['RIGHT']\n  ACTION_USE = action_map['USE']\n\n  # Define movement deltas for each action\n  MOVE_DELTAS = {\n      ACTION_UP: (0, -1),\n      ACTION_DOWN: (0, 1),\n      ACTION_LEFT: (-1, 0),\n      ACTION_RIGHT: (1, 0)\n  }\n\n  # Helper function to check if a given position is next to (or on) the target primitive\n  def is_next_to_target(check_pos, target_idx, current_grid):\n    px, py = check_pos\n    # Check the 3x3 neighborhood around the current position\n    for dx in [-1, 0, 1]:\n      for dy in [-1, 0, 1]:\n        nx, ny = px + dx, py + dy\n        # Ensure the neighbor position is within grid boundaries\n        if 0 <= nx < grid_width and 0 <= ny < grid_height:\n          # If the target primitive is found in this neighbor cell, return True\n          # We check if the count of the primitive at (nx, ny) is greater than 0\n          if current_grid[nx, ny, target_idx] > 0:\n            return True\n    return False\n\n  # Initialize BFS queue with the starting position and an empty path\n  q = collections.deque([(start_pos, [])]) # (current_position, list_of_actions_to_reach_current_position)\n  # Keep track of visited positions to avoid cycles and redundant exploration\n  visited = {start_pos}\n\n  # BFS loop\n  while q:\n    curr_pos, path = q.popleft()\n\n    # If the current position is next to (or on) the primitive, we've found a path\n    if is_next_to_target(curr_pos, primitive_idx, grid):\n      # Append the USE action and return the complete sequence of actions\n      return path + [ACTION_USE]\n\n    # Explore all possible moves from the current position\n    for action, delta in MOVE_DELTAS.items():\n      dx, dy = delta\n      next_x, next_y = curr_pos[0] + dx, curr_pos[1] + dy\n      next_pos = (next_x, next_y)\n\n      # Check if the next position is within grid boundaries\n      if not (0 <= next_x < grid_width and 0 <= next_y < grid_height):\n        continue\n\n      # If the next position has not been visited, add it to the queue\n      if next_pos not in visited:\n        visited.add(next_pos)\n        q.append((next_pos, path + [action]))\n\n  # If the BFS completes and no path is found (primitive is unreachable), return an empty list\n  return []", "island_id": 2, "scores": {"3": 0.5}}
{"timestamp": "2025-08-02T02:33:14.241277", "function_name": "collect", "function_body": "  try:\n    primitive_idx = env.world.cookbook.index.index(primitive)\n  except KeyError:\n    # If the primitive string is not known to the cookbook, it cannot be collected.\n    return []\n\n  # Check if the primitive is actually a grabbable item.\n  # The 'collect' function implies acquiring an item into inventory.\n  # If the primitive is marked as non-grabbable in the world configuration,\n  # it cannot be 'collected' in this manner, so no actions are returned.\n  if primitive_idx in env.world.non_grabbable_indices:\n    return []\n\n  # 2. Get current state details\n  current_state = env._current_state\n  grid = current_state.grid\n  start_pos = current_state.pos\n  grid_width, grid_height, _ = grid.shape\n\n  # 3. Get action mappings from the environment\n  action_map = env.action_specs()\n  ACTION_UP = action_map['UP']\n  ACTION_DOWN = action_map['DOWN']\n  ACTION_LEFT = action_map['LEFT']\n  ACTION_RIGHT = action_map['RIGHT']\n  ACTION_USE = action_map['USE']\n\n  # Define movement deltas for each action\n  MOVE_DELTAS = {\n      ACTION_UP: (0, -1),\n      ACTION_DOWN: (0, 1),\n      ACTION_LEFT: (-1, 0),\n      ACTION_RIGHT: (1, 0)\n  }\n\n  # Helper function to check if a given position is next to (or on) the target primitive\n  def is_next_to_target(check_pos, target_idx, current_grid):\n    px, py = check_pos\n    # Check the 3x3 neighborhood around the current position\n    for dx in [-1, 0, 1]:\n      for dy in [-1, 0, 1]:\n        nx, ny = px + dx, py + dy\n        # Ensure the neighbor position is within grid boundaries\n        if 0 <= nx < grid_width and 0 <= ny < grid_height:\n          # If the target primitive is found in this neighbor cell, return True\n          # We check if the count of the primitive at (nx, ny) is greater than 0\n          if current_grid[nx, ny, target_idx] > 0:\n            return True\n    return False\n\n  # Initialize BFS queue with the starting position and an empty path\n  q = collections.deque([(start_pos, [])]) # (current_position, list_of_actions_to_reach_current_position)\n  # Keep track of visited positions to avoid cycles and redundant exploration\n  visited = {start_pos}\n\n  # BFS loop\n  while q:\n    curr_pos, path = q.popleft()\n\n    # If the current position is next to (or on) the primitive, we've found a path\n    if is_next_to_target(curr_pos, primitive_idx, grid):\n      # Append the USE action and return the complete sequence of actions\n      return path + [ACTION_USE]\n\n    # Explore all possible moves from the current position\n    for action, delta in MOVE_DELTAS.items():\n      dx, dy = delta\n      next_x, next_y = curr_pos[0] + dx, curr_pos[1] + dy\n      next_pos = (next_x, next_y)\n\n      # Check if the next position is within grid boundaries\n      if not (0 <= next_x < grid_width and 0 <= next_y < grid_height):\n        continue\n\n      # If the next position has not been visited, add it to the queue\n      if next_pos not in visited:\n        visited.add(next_pos)\n        q.append((next_pos, path + [action]))\n\n  # If the BFS completes and no path is found (primitive is unreachable), return an empty list\n  return []", "island_id": 2, "scores": {"3": 0.5}}
{"timestamp": "2025-08-02T02:34:03.464850", "function_name": "collect", "function_body": "  state = env._current_state\n  cookbook = env.world.cookbook\n  \n  # 1. Get the integer index for the primitive.\n  # If the primitive name is not valid or not found in the cookbook's index, \n  # it cannot be collected, so return an empty list of actions.\n  try:\n    primitive_idx = cookbook.index[primitive]\n  except KeyError:\n    return []\n\n  # 2. Optimization: Check if the agent already possesses the primitive in its inventory.\n  # If the agent's inventory for this primitive is greater than 0, it means the item\n  # has already been collected or is available, so no further actions are needed.\n  if state.inventory[primitive_idx] > 0:\n    return []\n\n  grid = state.grid\n  agent_pos = state.pos  # The agent's current (x, y) coordinates.\n  \n  # 3. Retrieve the integer mappings for movement and interaction actions from the environment.\n  action_map = env.action_specs()\n  UP = action_map['UP']\n  DOWN = action_map['DOWN']\n  LEFT = action_map['LEFT']\n  RIGHT = action_map['RIGHT']\n  USE = action_map['USE']\n\n  # 4. A dictionary to map (delta_x, delta_y) movements to their corresponding action integers.\n  # This is crucial for reconstructing the path once the BFS finds a target.\n  dir_to_action = {\n      (0, -1): UP,    # Move Up (decreases Y coordinate)\n      (0, 1): DOWN,   # Move Down (increases Y coordinate)\n      (-1, 0): LEFT,  # Move Left (decreases X coordinate)\n      (1, 0): RIGHT,  # Move Right (increases X coordinate)\n  }\n\n  # 5. Get grid dimensions from the state's grid shape.\n  grid_width, grid_height, _ = grid.shape\n\n  # 6. Identify indices of entities that block agent movement (impassable terrain).\n  # Based on common simulation environments, 'BOUNDARY' and 'WATER' are typically\n  # impassable. We add them if they exist in the cookbook's index.\n  impassable_indices = set()\n  if 'BOUNDARY' in cookbook.index.contents:\n      impassable_indices.add(cookbook.index['BOUNDARY'])\n  if 'WATER' in cookbook.index.contents:\n      impassable_indices.add(cookbook.index['WATER'])\n\n  # 7. Helper function to determine if a given grid cell is traversable by the agent.\n  # A cell is traversable if it's within grid boundaries and does not contain any impassable entities.\n  def is_cell_traversable(x, y):\n      # Check if the coordinates are within the grid boundaries.\n      if not (0 <= x < grid_width and 0 <= y < grid_height):\n          return False  # Cell is out of bounds.\n\n      # Check if the cell contains any impassable entity.\n      for imp_idx in impassable_indices:\n          if grid[x, y, imp_idx] == 1:\n              return False  # Cell is blocked by an impassable object.\n      return True\n\n  # 8. Initial check: If the agent's current position is not traversable, it cannot move,\n  # so no path can be found.\n  if not is_cell_traversable(agent_pos[0], agent_pos[1]):\n      return []\n\n  # 9. Find all grid locations where the desired primitive currently exists.\n  # `np.argwhere` returns (row, col) which corresponds to (x, y) for grid coordinates.\n  primitive_locations = np.argwhere(grid[:, :, primitive_idx] == 1)\n\n  # 10. If the primitive is not found anywhere on the grid, return an empty list of actions.\n  if len(primitive_locations) == 0:\n    return []\n\n  # 11. Determine the set of valid 'target cells' for the BFS.\n  # The agent needs to reach a cell from which it can successfully 'USE' the primitive.\n  # The `CraftState.next_to` method implies the 'USE' action works for any primitive\n  # within a 3x3 neighborhood of the agent's position (including the agent's own cell).\n  bfs_target_cells = set()\n  for px, py in primitive_locations:\n    # Iterate through the 3x3 neighborhood around each primitive's location.\n    for dx_n in [-1, 0, 1]:\n      for dy_n in [-1, 0, 1]:\n        nx, ny = px + dx_n, py + dy_n\n        \n        # If this neighboring cell is traversable, it's a potential target for the agent.\n        if is_cell_traversable(nx, ny):\n          bfs_target_cells.add((nx, ny))\n\n  # 12. If no traversable cells are found around any primitive (e.g., all primitives are\n  # surrounded by impassable terrain), no path can be found.\n  if not bfs_target_cells:\n      return []\n\n  # 13. Perform Breadth-First Search (BFS) to find the shortest path from the agent's\n  # current position to any of the `bfs_target_cells`.\n  # The queue stores tuples: ((current_x, current_y), [list_of_actions_to_reach_here]).\n  q = collections.deque([(agent_pos, [])])\n  \n  # `visited` set tracks cells (coordinates) that have already been added to the queue\n  # to prevent cycles and redundant paths, ensuring the shortest path is found first.\n  visited = {agent_pos} \n\n  while q:\n    current_coords, current_path = q.popleft()\n    \n    # If the current position is one of our target cells, we've found the shortest path.\n    # This path leads to a cell from which the 'USE' action can be performed.\n    if current_coords in bfs_target_cells:\n      # Append the 'USE' action to the path and return the complete sequence.\n      return current_path + [USE]\n\n    # Explore the four cardinal neighbors (LEFT, RIGHT, UP, DOWN).\n    for dx, dy in [(-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)]: \n      next_x, next_y = current_coords[0] + dx, current_coords[1] + dy\n      next_coords = (next_x, next_y)\n\n      # Check if the next position is valid (within bounds, traversable, and not yet visited).\n      if is_cell_traversable(next_x, next_y) and next_coords not in visited:\n        visited.add(next_coords) # Mark as visited to avoid re-processing.\n        \n        # Create a new path by appending the action required to move to `next_coords`.\n        next_path = current_path + [dir_to_action[(dx, dy)]]\n        q.append((next_coords, next_path)) # Add the new state (position + path) to the queue.\n  \n  # 14. If the queue becomes empty and no path to any target cell was found,\n  # it means the primitive is unreachable from the current agent's position.\n  return []", "island_id": 7, "scores": {"3": 0.5}}
{"timestamp": "2025-08-02T02:34:04.712719", "function_name": "collect", "function_body": "  \"\"\"Improved version of `collect_v1`.\n\n    This function determines the shortest sequence of actions (movements and a final USE)\n    required for the agent to reach and interact with a specified primitive item in the environment.\n    It prioritizes items already in the inventory and then uses Breadth-First Search (BFS)\n    to find the closest instance of the primitive on the grid.\n\n    Args:\n        env (env.CraftLab): The CraftLab environment object, providing access to the current state.\n        primitive (str): The string name of the primitive item to collect (e.g., \"WOOD\", \"STONE\").\n\n    Returns:\n        List[int]: A list of integer action codes (e.g., UP, DOWN, LEFT, RIGHT, USE)\n                   representing the optimal path to collect the primitive. Returns an\n                   empty list if the primitive is already in inventory, not found,\n                   or unreachable.\n  \"\"\"\n  state = env._current_state\n  cookbook = env.world.cookbook\n  \n  # 1. Resolve Primitive Index: Convert the primitive's string name to its integer ID.\n  #    If the name is invalid or not in the cookbook, the primitive cannot be collected.\n  try:\n    primitive_idx = cookbook.index[primitive]\n  except KeyError:\n    return []\n\n  # 2. Inventory Check: If the agent already possesses the primitive, no action is needed.\n  if state.inventory[primitive_idx] > 0:\n    return []\n\n  grid = state.grid\n  agent_pos = state.pos  # Current (x, y) coordinates of the agent.\n  \n  # 3. Action Mappings: Retrieve integer codes for movement and interaction actions.\n  action_map = env.action_specs()\n  UP = action_map['UP']\n  DOWN = action_map['DOWN']\n  LEFT = action_map['LEFT']\n  RIGHT = action_map['RIGHT']\n  USE = action_map['USE']\n\n  # Map (dx, dy) movement vectors to their corresponding action integer.\n  # This is crucial for reconstructing the path from the BFS predecessor information.\n  dir_to_action = {\n      (0, -1): UP,   # Move Up (decreasing Y)\n      (0, 1): DOWN,  # Move Down (increasing Y)\n      (-1, 0): LEFT, # Move Left (decreasing X)\n      (1, 0): RIGHT, # Move Right (increasing X)\n  }\n\n  # 4. Grid Dimensions: Get the width and height of the grid.\n  grid_width, grid_height, _ = grid.shape\n\n  # 5. Identify Impassable Terrain: Collect indices of entities that block agent movement.\n  #    The agent cannot move into cells containing these entities.\n  impassable_names = ['BOUNDARY', 'WATER']\n  impassable_indices = {\n      cookbook.index[name] for name in impassable_names if name in cookbook.index.contents\n  }\n\n  # 6. Helper Function: Check if a given cell is valid for agent traversal.\n  def is_cell_traversable(x, y):\n      # Check if coordinates are within grid boundaries.\n      if not (0 <= x < grid_width and 0 <= y < grid_height):\n          return False  # Out of bounds.\n\n      # Check if the cell contains any impassable entity.\n      for imp_idx in impassable_indices:\n          # If the count of an impassable item at (x,y) is 1 (or more), it's blocked.\n          if grid[x, y, imp_idx] == 1: \n              return False  # Blocked by impassable terrain.\n      return True\n\n  # Initial check: Ensure the agent's current position is traversable.\n  # If not, it's stuck and cannot move.\n  if not is_cell_traversable(agent_pos[0], agent_pos[1]):\n      return []\n\n  # 7. Find Primitive Locations: Locate all instances of the desired primitive on the grid.\n  #    `np.argwhere` returns (x, y) coordinates where the primitive_idx layer is 1.\n  primitive_locations = np.argwhere(grid[:, :, primitive_idx] == 1)\n\n  # If no instances of the primitive are found on the grid, it's unreachable.\n  if len(primitive_locations) == 0:\n    return []\n\n  # 8. Define BFS Target Cells: These are traversable cells from which the agent can\n  #    successfully perform a 'USE' action to collect the primitive.\n  #    `CraftState.next_to` indicates a 3x3 neighborhood for interaction.\n  bfs_target_cells = set()\n  for px, py in primitive_locations:\n    # Iterate through the 3x3 neighborhood around each primitive.\n    for dx_n in [-1, 0, 1]:\n      for dy_n in [-1, 0, 1]:\n        nx, ny = px + dx_n, py + dy_n\n        \n        # A target cell must be within grid bounds AND traversable by the agent.\n        if is_cell_traversable(nx, ny):\n          bfs_target_cells.add((nx, ny))\n\n  # If no valid, traversable target cells exist around any primitive, it's unreachable.\n  if not bfs_target_cells:\n      return []\n\n  # 9. Breadth-First Search (BFS): Find the shortest path to any target cell.\n  #    The queue stores tuples: ((current_x, current_y), [list_of_actions_to_reach_here]).\n  q = collections.deque([(agent_pos, [])])\n  \n  # `visited` set keeps track of cells already processed to prevent cycles and redundant paths.\n  visited = {agent_pos} \n\n  while q:\n    current_coords, current_path = q.popleft()\n    \n    # If the current position is one of our target cells, we've found the shortest path.\n    if current_coords in bfs_target_cells:\n      # Append the 'USE' action as the final step and return the complete action sequence.\n      return current_path + [USE]\n\n    # Explore the four cardinal neighbors (Up, Down, Left, Right).\n    # The order of exploration doesn't affect path length for unweighted edges.\n    for dx, dy in [(-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)]: # Standard 4-directional moves\n      next_x, next_y = current_coords[0] + dx, current_coords[1] + dy\n      next_coords = (next_x, next_y)\n\n      # Check if the next position is valid (within grid, traversable, and not yet visited).\n      if is_cell_traversable(next_x, next_y) and next_coords not in visited:\n        visited.add(next_coords) # Mark this cell as visited.\n        \n        # Create the new path by appending the action required to move to `next_coords`.\n        next_path = current_path + [dir_to_action[(dx, dy)]]\n        q.append((next_coords, next_path)) # Add the new state to the queue.\n  \n  # 10. No Path Found: If the BFS queue becomes empty and no target cell was reached,\n  #     the primitive is unreachable from the current state.\n  return []", "island_id": 7, "scores": {"3": 0.5}}
