{"timestamp": "2025-08-02T09:59:52.783612", "function_name": "collect", "function_body": "  current_state = env._current_state\n  cookbook = env.world.cookbook\n  grid = current_state.grid \n  \n  # 1. Map the primitive name (string) to its integer ID.\n  primitive_idx = cookbook.index[primitive] \n  if primitive_idx is None:\n    # If the primitive name is not recognized, it cannot be collected.\n    return []\n\n  # 2. Early exit: If the primitive is already present in the agent's inventory,\n  # no collection actions are needed from the environment.\n  if current_state.inventory[primitive_idx] > 0:\n    return []\n\n  # 3. Get action mappings from the environment's action_specs for clarity.\n  action_map = env.action_specs()\n  ACTION_UP = action_map['UP']\n  ACTION_DOWN = action_map['DOWN']\n  ACTION_LEFT = action_map['LEFT']\n  ACTION_RIGHT = action_map['RIGHT']\n  ACTION_USE = action_map['USE']\n\n  # Define possible movement actions as (dx, dy, action_id) tuples.\n  MOVEMENT_ACTIONS = [\n      (0, -1, ACTION_UP),    # Move North (Up)\n      (0, 1, ACTION_DOWN),   # Move South (Down)\n      (-1, 0, ACTION_LEFT),  # Move West (Left)\n      (1, 0, ACTION_RIGHT),  # Move East (Right)\n  ]\n\n  # Get grid dimensions for boundary checks during pathfinding.\n  WIDTH, HEIGHT, _ = grid.shape\n\n  # Agent's starting position for the Breadth-First Search (BFS).\n  start_pos = current_state.pos\n\n  # Determine the collection mechanism based on primitive type.\n  # Grabbable items (e.g., WOOD, IRON, GRASS) require being next to them and using ACTION_USE.\n  # Stationary items (WORKSHOPs, WATER, STONE, BOUNDARY) require being on their cell.\n  is_grabbable = primitive_idx in env.world.grabbable_indices\n  is_workshop = primitive_idx in env.world.workshop_indices\n  is_water = primitive_idx == env.world.water_index\n  is_stone = primitive_idx == env.world.stone_index\n  \n  # Check if the *target primitive itself* is one of the non-grabbable, stationary types.\n  # This is important for movement logic: we can move onto these cells if they are our target.\n  is_target_stationary_non_grabbable = primitive_idx in env.world.non_grabbable_indices\n\n  # BFS initialization:\n  # The queue stores tuples: (current_position_tuple, list_of_actions_taken_to_reach_here).\n  q = collections.deque([(start_pos, [])]) \n  # The 'visited' set keeps track of grid positions already explored via a shortest path.\n  visited = {start_pos} \n\n  # Variables to store the shortest path found to a collection point.\n  # `min_total_actions_len` stores the length of the shortest *complete* path (movement + collection action).\n  # `shortest_path_actions` stores the actual sequence of actions for this best path.\n  min_total_actions_len = float('inf') \n  shortest_path_actions = []     \n\n  # BFS traversal loop.\n  while q:\n    current_pos, actions_to_current_pos = q.popleft()\n    cx, cy = current_pos # Unpack current position coordinates for direct grid access.\n\n    # Pruning 1 (after popping from queue):\n    # If the current path to this position, plus the *minimal* additional cost to collect\n    # (1 for 'USE' if grabbable, 0 otherwise), is already as long as or longer than\n    # the best complete collection path found so far, then this branch cannot lead to a shorter solution.\n    estimated_min_cost_from_current_pos = 1 if is_grabbable else 0 # Minimum additional steps from this position\n    if len(actions_to_current_pos) + estimated_min_cost_from_current_pos >= min_total_actions_len:\n        continue # Prune this path and its descendants.\n\n    # --- Check if the primitive can be collected from `current_pos` ---\n    can_collect_from_here = False\n    actions_to_complete_collection = [] # Additional actions needed *after* reaching `current_pos` (e.g., USE).\n\n    if is_grabbable:\n      # For grabbable items, the agent needs to be in the 3x3 neighborhood of the item.\n      # We check the grid around current_pos for the primitive's presence.\n      found_in_neighborhood = False\n      for dx_adj in [-1, 0, 1]:\n        for dy_adj in [-1, 0, 1]:\n          adj_x, adj_y = cx + dx_adj, cy + dy_adj\n          # Ensure adjacent cell is within grid boundaries.\n          if 0 <= adj_x < WIDTH and 0 <= adj_y < HEIGHT:\n            # Check if the primitive is present in the adjacent cell.\n            if grid[adj_x, adj_y, primitive_idx] > 0:\n              found_in_neighborhood = True\n              break # Primitive found in neighborhood, no need to check other adjacent cells.\n        if found_in_neighborhood:\n            break # Break outer loop too.\n      \n      if found_in_neighborhood:\n        can_collect_from_here = True\n        actions_to_complete_collection = [ACTION_USE] # 'USE' action is required for grabbables.\n    elif is_target_stationary_non_grabbable: # Covers workshops, water, stone, boundary if they are the target\n      # For stationary items, the agent needs to be directly on the cell.\n      # Check if the primitive is present at the current exact cell.\n      if grid[cx, cy, primitive_idx] > 0:\n        can_collect_from_here = True\n        actions_to_complete_collection = [] # No 'USE' action needed for these.\n    \n    # If the primitive can be collected from `current_pos`, update the shortest path found.\n    if can_collect_from_here:\n      current_full_path_len = len(actions_to_current_pos) + len(actions_to_complete_collection)\n      \n      # If this path is strictly shorter than the best found so far, update the best.\n      # This handles cases where multiple paths lead to the same collection point, or different\n      # collection points exist, ensuring we find the shortest overall.\n      if current_full_path_len < min_total_actions_len:\n        min_total_actions_len = current_full_path_len\n        shortest_path_actions = actions_to_current_pos + actions_to_complete_collection\n      # We do NOT `break` here. BFS must continue to guarantee the shortest path across *all* possible collection points.\n      # The pruning logic handles cutting off longer branches efficiently.\n\n    # --- Explore valid neighbors (movement actions) ---\n    for dx, dy, move_action in MOVEMENT_ACTIONS:\n      new_x, new_y = cx + dx, cy + dy\n      new_pos = (new_x, new_y)\n\n      # Check if the new position is within grid boundaries.\n      if 0 <= new_x < WIDTH and 0 <= new_y < HEIGHT:\n        # Check for impassable terrain.\n        # An agent cannot move into a cell occupied by any non-grabbable entity,\n        # UNLESS that non-grabbable entity is the *target primitive itself*\n        # (e.g., if we are trying to reach a 'WORKSHOP' or 'WATER').\n        is_new_pos_blocked_by_obstacle = False\n        for obstacle_kind_idx in env.world.non_grabbable_indices:\n            # If the current obstacle we're checking is actually the primitive we want to collect,\n            # then it's not an obstacle for *movement* to that cell.\n            if obstacle_kind_idx == primitive_idx:\n                continue \n            \n            # Check if the potential new position is occupied by this obstacle kind.\n            if grid[new_x, new_y, obstacle_kind_idx] > 0:\n                is_new_pos_blocked_by_obstacle = True\n                break # Found an impassable obstacle, no need to check others.\n        \n        if is_new_pos_blocked_by_obstacle:\n            continue # This path is blocked, skip to the next neighbor.\n\n        # Check if this position has not been visited yet through a shorter or equal path.\n        if new_pos not in visited:\n          # Pruning 2 (before adding to queue):\n          # Estimate the total path length if we were to reach `new_pos` and\n          # then immediately collect (assuming `new_pos` is itself a collection point, or adjacent for grabbables).\n          # Cost to reach `new_pos` is `len(actions_to_current_pos) + 1`.\n          # Estimated minimal cost from `new_pos` to collect is `1` for grabbable, `0` for others.\n          estimated_total_path_via_new_pos = len(actions_to_current_pos) + 1 + (1 if is_grabbable else 0)\n          \n          # Only add `new_pos` to the queue if its estimated total path length\n          # is strictly shorter than the best complete path found so far.\n          if estimated_total_path_via_new_pos < min_total_actions_len: \n              visited.add(new_pos) # Mark `new_pos` as visited.\n              q.append((new_pos, actions_to_current_pos + [move_action])) # Enqueue `new_pos` and its path.\n  \n  # After BFS completes, `shortest_path_actions` will contain the shortest sequence of actions\n  # to collect the primitive, or an empty list if unreachable.\n  return shortest_path_actions", "island_id": 3, "scores": {"3": 0.0}}
{"timestamp": "2025-08-02T10:00:36.928141", "function_name": "collect", "function_body": "  current_state = env._current_state\n  grid = current_state.grid\n  agent_pos = current_state.pos  # Agent's current (x, y) position\n  cookbook = env.world.cookbook\n  \n  # --- 1. Preprocessing: Retrieve necessary IDs and action mappings ---\n  # Convert the string name of the primitive to its integer ID used in the game state.\n  try:\n    primitive_idx = cookbook.index[primitive]\n  except KeyError:\n    # If the primitive name is not found in the cookbook's index, it's unknown.\n    return []\n\n  # Get numerical action codes from the environment's action specification.\n  action_map = env.action_specs()\n  ACTION_UP = action_map['UP']\n  ACTION_DOWN = action_map['DOWN']\n  ACTION_LEFT = action_map['LEFT']\n  ACTION_RIGHT = action_map['RIGHT']\n  ACTION_USE = action_map['USE']\n\n  # Define relative position changes (dx, dy) for each movement action.\n  movement_deltas = {\n      ACTION_UP: (0, -1),   # Up: Y-coordinate decreases\n      ACTION_DOWN: (0, 1),    # Down: Y-coordinate increases\n      ACTION_LEFT: (-1, 0),   # Left: X-coordinate decreases\n      ACTION_RIGHT: (1, 0),   # Right: X-coordinate increases\n  }\n\n  # Get grid dimensions for boundary checks.\n  grid_width, grid_height, _ = grid.shape \n\n  # Identify entity types that act as impassable barriers for agent movement.\n  # These typically include \"WATER\" and \"BOUNDARY\" in CraftWorld.\n  impassable_kind_indices = set()\n  if 'WATER' in cookbook.index.contents:\n    impassable_kind_indices.add(cookbook.index['WATER'])\n  if 'BOUNDARY' in cookbook.index.contents:\n    impassable_kind_indices.add(cookbook.index['BOUNDARY'])\n  \n  # --- 2. Helper Function: Check Cell Traversability ---\n  def is_traversable(x: int, y: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if a given grid coordinate is within bounds and free of impassable entities.\"\"\"\n    # Check if the coordinates are within the grid boundaries.\n    if not (0 <= x < grid_width and 0 <= y < grid_height):\n      return False\n    \n    # Check if the cell is occupied by any entity marked as impassable.\n    for impassable_idx in impassable_kind_indices:\n      # If grid[x, y, impassable_idx] is 1, it means the impassable kind is present.\n      if grid[x, y, impassable_idx] == 1:\n        return False # This cell is an obstacle.\n    return True\n\n  # --- 3. Identify Primitive Locations and Valid \"Use\" Target Cells ---\n  # Find all grid cells where instances of the target primitive are located.\n  primitive_locations_on_grid = np.argwhere(grid[:, :, primitive_idx] == 1)\n\n  if len(primitive_locations_on_grid) == 0:\n    # If the primitive is not present anywhere on the current grid, it cannot be collected.\n    return []\n\n  # Determine all valid \"target cells\" from which the agent can successfully\n  # apply the 'USE' action to collect the primitive. A cell is a valid \"use\" target if:\n  #   a) It is located within the 3x3 Chebyshev neighborhood (distance <= 1) of any\n  #      primitive instance on the grid.\n  #   b) It is traversable by the agent.\n  valid_use_spots = np.full((grid_width, grid_height), False, dtype=bool)\n  for px, py in primitive_locations_on_grid:\n    # Explore the 3x3 neighborhood around each primitive instance (px, py).\n    for dx_use in [-1, 0, 1]:\n      for dy_use in [-1, 0, 1]:\n        # Calculate the neighboring cell coordinates, which could be an agent's \"use\" spot.\n        ux, uy = px + dx_use, py + dy_use \n        \n        # Mark (ux, uy) as a valid `USE` spot if it's within grid bounds\n        # AND it is a traversable cell for the agent.\n        if is_traversable(ux, uy):\n          valid_use_spots[ux, uy] = True\n  \n  # If no traversable \"use\" targets are found, the primitive is unreachable.\n  if not np.any(valid_use_spots):\n      return []\n\n  # --- 4. Immediate Check: Is Agent Already at a Valid \"Use\" Target? ---\n  # If the agent is already positioned correctly (adjacent to or on the primitive),\n  # only the 'USE' action is required.\n  if valid_use_spots[agent_pos[0], agent_pos[1]]:\n    return [ACTION_USE]\n\n  # --- 5. Pathfinding (BFS): Find the Shortest Path to Any Valid \"Use\" Target ---\n  # BFS queue: Stores tuples of ((current_x, current_y), [actions_taken_to_reach_here]).\n  queue = collections.deque([(agent_pos, [])])\n  \n  # Visited set: Tracks grid cells that have already been explored during BFS\n  # to prevent cycles and ensure the shortest path.\n  visited_cells = {agent_pos}\n\n  while queue:\n    (cx, cy), current_path = queue.popleft() # Dequeue the current position and its path.\n\n    # Explore all four cardinal neighbors (Up, Down, Left, Right) from the current cell.\n    for action_type, (dx, dy) in movement_deltas.items():\n      nx, ny = cx + dx, cy + dy  # Calculate the coordinates of the new potential position.\n      next_position = (nx, ny)\n\n      # Check if the new position is valid for movement and has not been visited yet.\n      if is_traversable(nx, ny) and next_position not in visited_cells:\n        \n        # If this new position is one of our designated 'valid_use_spots',\n        # we have found the shortest path to a spot from which we can 'USE'.\n        if valid_use_spots[nx, ny]:\n          # Return the sequence of movement actions to reach `next_position`,\n          # followed by the 'USE' action.\n          return current_path + [action_type, ACTION_USE] \n        else:\n          # If the new position is not yet a target, add it to the queue for further\n          # exploration and mark it as visited.\n          visited_cells.add(next_position)\n          queue.append((next_position, current_path + [action_type]))\n        \n  # --- 6. Unreachable Primitive ---\n  # If the BFS queue becomes empty without finding a path, the primitive is unreachable.\n  return []", "island_id": 9, "scores": {"3": 0.5}}
{"timestamp": "2025-08-02T10:00:38.108425", "function_name": "collect", "function_body": "  current_state = env._current_state\n  grid = current_state.grid\n  agent_pos = current_state.pos  # Agent's (x, y) tuple position\n  cookbook = env.world.cookbook\n  \n  # 1. Map the primitive name to its integer ID using the cookbook's index.\n  try:\n    primitive_idx = cookbook.index[primitive]\n  except KeyError:\n    # If the primitive name is not recognized, it cannot be collected.\n    return []\n\n  # 2. Get integer mappings for all relevant actions from the environment's action space.\n  action_map = env.action_specs()\n  ACTION_UP = action_map['UP']\n  ACTION_DOWN = action_map['DOWN']\n  ACTION_LEFT = action_map['LEFT']\n  ACTION_RIGHT = action_map['RIGHT']\n  ACTION_USE = action_map['USE']\n\n  # 3. IMMEDIATE CHECK: If the agent is already in a position to use the primitive,\n  # return only the USE action. This leverages the CraftState's `next_to` method,\n  # which efficiently checks the 3x3 neighborhood around the agent for the primitive.\n  if current_state.next_to(primitive_idx):\n    return [ACTION_USE]\n\n  # If not immediately next to, proceed with pathfinding.\n\n  # 4. Define movement actions and their corresponding (dx, dy) deltas.\n  movement_actions_map = {\n      ACTION_UP: (0, -1),   # Moving UP decreases Y-coordinate\n      ACTION_DOWN: (0, 1),    # Moving DOWN increases Y-coordinate\n      ACTION_LEFT: (-1, 0),   # Moving LEFT decreases X-coordinate\n      ACTION_RIGHT: (1, 0),   # Moving RIGHT increases X-coordinate\n  }\n\n  # 5. Get grid dimensions (width, height, number of kinds).\n  grid_width, grid_height, _ = grid.shape\n\n  # 6. Identify terrain types that block agent movement.\n  # In CraftWorld, \"WATER\" and \"BOUNDARY\" are typically impassable obstacles.\n  impassable_kind_indices = set()\n  if 'WATER' in cookbook.index.contents:\n    impassable_kind_indices.add(cookbook.index['WATER'])\n  if 'BOUNDARY' in cookbook.index.contents:\n    impassable_kind_indices.add(cookbook.index['BOUNDARY'])\n  \n  # Helper function: Determines if a given grid cell is within bounds and traversable.\n  def is_traversable(x: int, y: int) -> bool:\n    # Check if coordinates are within the grid boundaries.\n    if not (0 <= x < grid_width and 0 <= y < grid_height):\n      return False\n    \n    # Check if the cell contains any impassable kind.\n    for impassable_idx in impassable_kind_indices:\n      if grid[x, y, impassable_idx] == 1: # If this specific impassable kind is present at (x,y)\n        return False\n    return True\n\n  # 7. Locate all instances of the target primitive on the grid.\n  # np.argwhere returns a list of (x, y) coordinates where the condition is true.\n  primitive_locations_on_grid = np.argwhere(grid[:, :, primitive_idx] == 1)\n\n  if len(primitive_locations_on_grid) == 0:\n    # If the primitive is not found anywhere on the grid, it's impossible to collect it.\n    return []\n\n  # 8. Pre-calculate all traversable grid cells from which the agent can successfully `USE`\n  # the target primitive. An agent can `USE` a primitive if it's in an\n  # adjacent cell (including diagonals, i.e., Chebyshev distance <= 1).\n  valid_use_spots = np.full((grid_width, grid_height), False, dtype=bool)\n  for px, py in primitive_locations_on_grid:\n    # Iterate through the 3x3 neighborhood around each primitive location.\n    for dx_use in [-1, 0, 1]:\n      for dy_use in [-1, 0, 1]:\n        ux, uy = px + dx_use, py + dy_use # Candidate position for the agent to stand on\n        \n        # Mark (ux, uy) as a valid `USE` spot if it's within grid bounds\n        # AND it is a traversable cell for the agent.\n        if is_traversable(ux, uy):\n          valid_use_spots[ux, uy] = True\n  \n  # If after checking all primitive locations, no traversable \"use\" spots are found,\n  # it means the primitive is effectively unreachable (e.g., surrounded by water).\n  if not np.any(valid_use_spots):\n    return []\n\n  # 9. Pathfinding using Breadth-First Search (BFS).\n  # BFS finds the shortest path in an unweighted graph (our grid).\n  # The queue stores tuples: ((current_x, current_y), list_of_actions_taken_to_reach_here).\n  bfs_queue = collections.deque([(agent_pos, [])])\n  \n  # `visited_cells` set keeps track of grid cells already added to the queue to prevent cycles\n  # and redundant exploration, ensuring the shortest path is found first.\n  visited_cells = {agent_pos} \n  \n  while bfs_queue:\n    (current_x, current_y), actions_to_current = bfs_queue.popleft()\n\n    # Explore all four possible cardinal movement directions from the current cell.\n    for action_type, (dx, dy) in movement_actions_map.items():\n      next_x, next_y = current_x + dx, current_y + dy  # Calculate potential next position\n      next_pos = (next_x, next_y)\n\n      # Check if the next position is traversable and has not been visited yet.\n      if is_traversable(next_x, next_y) and next_pos not in visited_cells:\n        \n        # If the next position is a `valid_use_spot`, a path has been found.\n        # Since this is BFS, this path is guaranteed to be the shortest.\n        if valid_use_spots[next_x, next_y]:\n          # Return the actions to reach `next_pos`, followed by the `ACTION_USE`.\n          return actions_to_current + [action_type, ACTION_USE] \n        else:\n          # If `next_pos` is not a `valid_use_spot` yet, add this new position to the queue\n          # for further exploration and mark it as visited.\n          visited_cells.add(next_pos)\n          bfs_queue.append((next_pos, actions_to_current + [action_type]))\n        \n  # 10. If the BFS completes and no path to a `valid_use_spot` was found,\n  # it means the primitive is unreachable from the agent's current position under current conditions.\n  return []", "island_id": 9, "scores": {"3": 0.5}}
{"timestamp": "2025-08-02T10:01:38.189794", "function_name": "collect", "function_body": "  state = env._current_state\n  cookbook = env.world.cookbook\n  grid = state.grid # The grid is static for the pathfinding within this function call.\n  \n  # 1. Resolve the primitive name to its unique integer ID.\n  primitive_idx = cookbook.index[primitive] \n  if primitive_idx is None:\n    # If the primitive name is not found in the environment's cookbook, it cannot be collected.\n    return []\n\n  # 2. Map action names to their integer indices for clarity and direct use.\n  action_map = env.action_specs()\n  ACTION_UP = action_map['UP']\n  ACTION_DOWN = action_map['DOWN']\n  ACTION_LEFT = action_map['LEFT']\n  ACTION_RIGHT = action_map['RIGHT']\n  ACTION_USE = action_map['USE']\n\n  # 3. Obtain grid dimensions for boundary checks.\n  WIDTH, HEIGHT, _ = grid.shape\n\n  # 4. Define direction vectors: How agent's facing direction (0=UP, 1=DOWN, 2=LEFT, 3=RIGHT)\n  # translates to (dx, dy) changes for the cell directly in front.\n  DIR_VECTORS = {\n      0: (0, -1), # UP: (x, y-1)\n      1: (0, 1),  # DOWN: (x, y+1)\n      2: (-1, 0), # LEFT: (x-1, y)\n      3: (1, 0)   # RIGHT: (x+1, y)\n  }\n\n  # 5. Determine the collection mechanism based on the primitive type.\n  # Grabbable items (e.g., WOOD, IRON) require an agent to be adjacent, facing it, and using ACTION_USE.\n  # Non-grabbable items (e.g., WORKSHOP, WATER, STONE, BOUNDARY) require the agent to be on the same cell.\n  is_grabbable = primitive_idx in env.world.grabbable_indices\n  is_workshop = primitive_idx in env.world.workshop_indices\n  is_stationary_primitive = (\n      primitive_idx == env.world.water_index or \n      primitive_idx == env.world.stone_index or \n      primitive_idx == cookbook.index['BOUNDARY']\n  )\n  \n  # 6. Optimization: If the primitive is grabbable and already in inventory, no actions are needed.\n  # This prevents unnecessary pathfinding if the goal is already satisfied.\n  if is_grabbable and state.inventory[primitive_idx] > 0:\n    return []\n\n  # 7. Helper function to check if a (position, direction) state allows for collection and what final actions are needed.\n  def _get_collection_actions(current_pos, current_dir):\n      \"\"\"\n      Checks if the primitive can be collected from the given (position, direction) and\n      returns (True, list_of_final_actions) if so, else (False, []).\n      \"\"\"\n      if is_grabbable:\n          # For grabbable items, the agent must be directly facing the item.\n          dx, dy = DIR_VECTORS[current_dir]\n          fwd_x, fwd_y = current_pos[0] + dx, current_pos[1] + dy\n          \n          # Check if the cell in front is within grid boundaries and contains the primitive.\n          if 0 <= fwd_x < WIDTH and 0 <= fwd_y < HEIGHT:\n              if grid[fwd_x, fwd_y, primitive_idx] > 0:\n                  return True, [ACTION_USE] # 'USE' is the action to grab.\n          return False, []\n      else: # For non-grabbable items (workshops, water, stone, boundary, etc.)\n          # Collection means simply being on the same cell as the primitive.\n          if grid[current_pos[0], current_pos[1], primitive_idx] > 0:\n              return True, [] # No additional action is needed.\n          return False, []\n\n  # 8. Initialize Breadth-First Search (BFS).\n  # Queue stores (current_position, current_direction, list_of_actions_taken_to_reach_here).\n  q = collections.deque([(state.pos, state.dir, [])]) \n  \n  # Visited set tracks (position, direction) pairs to prevent redundant computations and infinite loops.\n  visited = {(state.pos, state.dir)} \n\n  # Variables to store the shortest path found so far.\n  min_total_actions_len = float('inf')\n  optimal_action_sequence = []\n\n  # 9. BFS Loop: Continues as long as there are states to explore.\n  while q:\n    current_pos, current_dir, actions_to_current_state = q.popleft()\n\n    # 10. Pruning Optimization: If the current path length (to reach `current_pos`, `current_dir`)\n    # is already longer than or equal to the shortest complete path found, this branch cannot lead\n    # to a shorter solution, so we skip it.\n    if len(actions_to_current_state) >= min_total_actions_len:\n        continue\n\n    # 11. Check if the primitive can be collected from the current agent state.\n    can_collect, final_collection_actions = _get_collection_actions(current_pos, current_dir)\n    \n    # 12. If the primitive is collectable from this state, update the best path found so far.\n    if can_collect:\n      total_path_length = len(actions_to_current_state) + len(final_collection_actions)\n      \n      # If this path (including the final collection action) is shorter, update the optimal sequence.\n      if total_path_length < min_total_actions_len:\n        min_total_actions_len = total_path_length\n        optimal_action_sequence = actions_to_current_state + final_collection_actions\n      \n      # IMPORTANT: Do NOT `return` here. BFS finds the shortest path to a *state*.\n      # However, the overall task completion might involve an additional action (e.g., 'USE').\n      # A path reaching a state later in the BFS might still result in a shorter *total*\n      # sequence if it requires fewer final collection actions. We must continue exploring\n      # until all possible shorter paths are exhausted or pruned.\n\n    # 13. Explore valid neighbors by simulating movement actions (UP, DOWN, LEFT, RIGHT).\n    for move_action in [ACTION_UP, ACTION_DOWN, ACTION_LEFT, ACTION_RIGHT]:\n      # To accurately determine the next state after a move, we use a temporary `CraftState`\n      # and its `step` method. This handles environment rules like collisions, boundaries, etc.\n      \n      # Create a temporary `CraftState` reflecting the current BFS node's state.\n      # The grid and inventory remain constant for the pathfinding within this function call.\n      temp_sim_state = craft.CraftState(\n          state.scenario, state.grid, current_pos, current_dir, state.inventory\n      )\n      \n      # Simulate the movement action. The reward is not relevant for pathfinding.\n      _, next_sim_state = temp_sim_state.step(move_action)\n      \n      # Extract the new position and direction from the simulated state.\n      next_pos = next_sim_state.pos\n      next_dir = next_sim_state.dir\n\n      # Define the unique key for the new state to track in the `visited` set.\n      next_state_key = (next_pos, next_dir) \n\n      # If this new (position, direction) state has not been visited yet,\n      # add it to the queue for future exploration and mark it as visited.\n      if next_state_key not in visited:\n        visited.add(next_state_key)\n        q.append((next_pos, next_dir, actions_to_current_state + [move_action]))\n  \n  # 14. After the BFS completes, `optimal_action_sequence` will contain the shortest sequence\n  # of actions to collect the primitive. If no path was found, it will be an empty list.\n  return optimal_action_sequence", "island_id": 1, "scores": {"3": 1.0}}
{"timestamp": "2025-08-02T10:01:39.382250", "function_name": "collect", "function_body": "  current_state = env._current_state\n  cookbook = env.world.cookbook\n  grid = current_state.grid # The grid is static for pathfinding within this function call.\n  \n  # 1. Resolve the primitive name to its unique integer ID.\n  # Using .get() ensures a None return if the primitive name is not found, preventing KeyError.\n  primitive_idx = cookbook.index.get(primitive) \n  if primitive_idx is None:\n    # If the primitive name is not found in the environment's cookbook, it cannot be collected.\n    return []\n\n  # 2. Map action names to their integer indices for clarity and direct use.\n  action_map = env.action_specs()\n  ACTION_UP = action_map['UP']\n  ACTION_DOWN = action_map['DOWN']\n  ACTION_LEFT = action_map['LEFT']\n  ACTION_RIGHT = action_map['RIGHT']\n  ACTION_USE = action_map['USE']\n\n  # 3. Obtain grid dimensions for boundary checks.\n  WIDTH, HEIGHT, _ = grid.shape\n\n  # 4. Define direction vectors: How agent's facing direction (0=UP, 1=DOWN, 2=LEFT, 3=RIGHT)\n  # translates to (dx, dy) changes for the cell directly in front.\n  DIR_VECTORS = {\n      0: (0, -1), # UP: (x, y-1)\n      1: (0, 1),  # DOWN: (x, y+1)\n      2: (-1, 0), # LEFT: (x-1, y)\n      3: (1, 0)   # RIGHT: (x+1, y)\n  }\n\n  # 5. Determine the collection mechanism based on the primitive type.\n  # Grabbable items (e.g., WOOD, IRON) require an agent to be adjacent, facing it, and using ACTION_USE.\n  # Non-grabbable items (e.g., WORKSHOP, WATER, STONE, BOUNDARY) require the agent to be on the same cell.\n  is_grabbable = primitive_idx in env.world.grabbable_indices\n  \n  # 6. Optimization: If the primitive is grabbable and already in inventory, no actions are needed.\n  # This prevents unnecessary pathfinding if the goal is already satisfied.\n  if is_grabbable and current_state.inventory[primitive_idx] > 0:\n    return []\n\n  # 7. Helper function to check if a (position, direction) state allows for collection and what final actions are needed.\n  def _get_collection_info(pos, direction):\n      \"\"\"\n      Checks if the primitive can be collected from the given (position, direction) and\n      returns (is_collectable, list_of_final_actions) if so, else (False, []).\n      \"\"\"\n      if is_grabbable:\n          # For grabbable items, the agent must be directly facing the item and then use 'USE'.\n          dx, dy = DIR_VECTORS[direction]\n          fwd_x, fwd_y = pos[0] + dx, pos[1] + dy\n          \n          # Check if the cell in front is within grid boundaries and contains the primitive.\n          if 0 <= fwd_x < WIDTH and 0 <= fwd_y < HEIGHT and grid[fwd_x, fwd_y, primitive_idx] > 0:\n              return True, [ACTION_USE] # 'USE' is the action to grab.\n      else: # For non-grabbable items (workshops, water, stone, boundary etc.)\n          # Collection means simply being on the same cell as the primitive.\n          if grid[pos[0], pos[1], primitive_idx] > 0:\n              return True, [] # No additional action is needed.\n      \n      return False, []\n\n  # 8. Initialize Breadth-First Search (BFS).\n  # Queue stores (current_position, current_direction, list_of_actions_taken_to_reach_here).\n  q = collections.deque([(current_state.pos, current_state.dir, [])]) \n  \n  # Visited set tracks (position, direction) pairs to prevent redundant computations and infinite loops.\n  # A state is defined by (position, direction) because the outcome of 'USE' depends on direction.\n  visited = {(current_state.pos, current_state.dir)} \n\n  # Variables to store the shortest path found so far.\n  min_total_actions_len = float('inf')\n  optimal_action_sequence = []\n\n  # 9. BFS Loop: Continues as long as there are states to explore.\n  while q:\n    current_pos, current_dir, actions_to_current_state = q.popleft()\n\n    # 10. Pruning Optimization: If the current path length (to reach `current_pos`, `current_dir`)\n    # is already longer than or equal to the shortest complete path found, this branch cannot lead\n    # to a shorter solution, so we skip it. This helps reduce unnecessary exploration.\n    if len(actions_to_current_state) >= min_total_actions_len:\n        continue\n\n    # 11. Check if the primitive can be collected from the current agent state.\n    can_collect, final_collection_actions = _get_collection_info(current_pos, current_dir)\n    \n    # 12. If the primitive is collectable from this state, update the best path found so far.\n    if can_collect:\n      total_path_length = len(actions_to_current_state) + len(final_collection_actions)\n      \n      # If this path (including the final collection action) is shorter, update the optimal sequence.\n      if total_path_length < min_total_actions_len:\n        min_total_actions_len = total_path_length\n        optimal_action_sequence = actions_to_current_state + final_collection_actions\n      \n      # IMPORTANT: Do NOT `return` here immediately. BFS finds the shortest path to a *state*.\n      # However, the overall task completion might involve an additional action (e.g., 'USE').\n      # A path reaching a state later in the BFS might still result in a shorter *total*\n      # sequence if it requires fewer final collection actions. We must continue exploring\n      # until all possible shorter paths are exhausted or pruned to guarantee global optimum.\n\n    # 13. Explore valid neighbors by simulating movement actions (UP, DOWN, LEFT, RIGHT).\n    for move_action in [ACTION_UP, ACTION_DOWN, ACTION_LEFT, ACTION_RIGHT]:\n      # To accurately determine the next state after a move, we must use a temporary `CraftState`\n      # and its `step` method. This handles environment rules like collisions, boundaries,\n      # and how the agent's direction might change (e.g., automatically turning to face movement direction).\n      \n      # Create a temporary `CraftState` reflecting the current BFS node's state.\n      # The grid and inventory (for movement purposes) remain constant for pathfinding within this function call.\n      temp_sim_state = craft.CraftState(\n          current_state.scenario, current_state.grid, current_pos, current_dir, current_state.inventory\n      )\n      \n      # Simulate the movement action. The reward is not relevant for pathfinding here.\n      _, next_sim_state = temp_sim_state.step(move_action)\n      \n      # Extract the new position and direction from the simulated state.\n      next_pos = next_sim_state.pos\n      next_dir = next_sim_state.dir\n\n      # Define the unique key for the new state to track in the `visited` set.\n      next_state_key = (next_pos, next_dir) \n\n      # If this new (position, direction) state has not been visited yet,\n      # add it to the queue for future exploration and mark it as visited.\n      if next_state_key not in visited:\n        visited.add(next_state_key)\n        q.append((next_pos, next_dir, actions_to_current_state + [move_action]))\n  \n  # 14. After the BFS completes, `optimal_action_sequence` will contain the shortest sequence\n  # of actions to collect the primitive. If no path was found, it will remain an empty list.\n  return optimal_action_sequence", "island_id": 1, "scores": {"3": -1}}
{"timestamp": "2025-08-02T10:02:36.728534", "function_name": "collect", "function_body": "  current_state = env._current_state\n  cookbook = env.world.cookbook\n  grid = current_state.grid # Access the current grid for primitive locations\n  \n  # 1. Map the primitive name (string) to its integer ID.\n  primitive_idx = cookbook.index[primitive] \n  if primitive_idx is None:\n    # If the primitive name is not recognized, it cannot be collected.\n    return []\n\n  # 2. Early exit: If the primitive is already present in the agent's inventory,\n  # no collection actions are needed from the environment.\n  if current_state.inventory[primitive_idx] > 0:\n    return []\n\n  # 3. Get action mappings from the environment's action_specs for clarity.\n  action_map = env.action_specs()\n  ACTION_UP = action_map['UP']\n  ACTION_DOWN = action_map['DOWN']\n  ACTION_LEFT = action_map['LEFT']\n  ACTION_RIGHT = action_map['RIGHT']\n  ACTION_USE = action_map['USE']\n\n  # Define possible movement actions as (dx, dy, action_id) tuples.\n  # These define the change in coordinates and the corresponding action index.\n  MOVEMENT_DIRECTIONS = [\n      (0, -1, ACTION_UP),    # Move North (decreases Y)\n      (0, 1, ACTION_DOWN),   # Move South (increases Y)\n      (-1, 0, ACTION_LEFT),  # Move West (decreases X)\n      (1, 0, ACTION_RIGHT),  # Move East (increases X)\n  ]\n\n  # Get grid dimensions for boundary checks during pathfinding.\n  WIDTH, HEIGHT, _ = grid.shape\n\n  # Agent's starting position for the Breadth-First Search (BFS).\n  start_pos = current_state.pos\n\n  # Determine the collection mechanism based on primitive type.\n  # - `is_grabbable`: Items like WOOD, IRON, GRASS. Requires being next to them and using ACTION_USE.\n  # - `is_stationary_target`: Items like WORKSHOPs, WATER, STONE, BOUNDARY. Requires being directly on their cell.\n  is_grabbable = primitive_idx in env.world.grabbable_indices\n  is_workshop = primitive_idx in env.world.workshop_indices\n  is_water_stone = (primitive_idx == env.world.water_index or primitive_idx == env.world.stone_index)\n  \n  # Identify the index for 'BOUNDARY' for movement blocking logic.\n  boundary_kind_idx = cookbook.index['BOUNDARY']\n  # Flag to indicate if the target primitive itself is 'BOUNDARY'.\n  # This is crucial for movement logic: you can move onto a BOUNDARY if you're trying to collect it.\n  is_target_boundary = (primitive_idx == boundary_kind_idx)\n\n  # BFS initialization:\n  # The queue stores tuples: (current_position_tuple, list_of_actions_taken_to_reach_here).\n  q = collections.deque([(start_pos, [])]) \n  # The 'visited' set keeps track of grid positions already explored via a shortest path.\n  # Stores only the position (x, y) as we only care about shortest path to a location.\n  visited = {start_pos} \n\n  # Variables to store the shortest path found to a collection point.\n  # `min_total_actions_len` stores the length of the shortest *complete* path (movement + collection action).\n  # `shortest_path_actions` stores the actual sequence of actions for this best path.\n  min_total_actions_len = float('inf') \n  shortest_path_actions = []     \n\n  # BFS traversal loop to find the shortest path to a collectable primitive.\n  while q:\n    current_pos, actions_to_current_pos = q.popleft()\n    cx, cy = current_pos # Unpack current position coordinates.\n\n    # Pruning 1 (after popping from queue):\n    # If the current path length to this position, plus the *minimal* additional cost to collect\n    # (1 for 'USE' if grabbable, 0 otherwise), is already as long as or longer than\n    # the best complete collection path found so far, then this branch cannot lead to a shorter solution.\n    estimated_min_cost_to_collect = 1 if is_grabbable else 0\n    if len(actions_to_current_pos) + estimated_min_cost_to_collect >= min_total_actions_len:\n        continue # Prune this path and its descendants.\n\n    # Create a hypothetical CraftState at the current BFS position to use helper methods like next_to.\n    # Agent's direction and inventory are kept constant as they don't affect pathfinding geometry\n    # or the presence of items on the grid.\n    temp_state_at_pos = craft.CraftState(current_state.scenario, current_state.grid, current_pos, current_state.dir, current_state.inventory)\n\n    # --- Check if the primitive can be collected from `current_pos` ---\n    can_collect_from_here = False\n    actions_to_complete_collection = [] # Additional actions needed *after* reaching `current_pos` (e.g., USE).\n\n    if is_grabbable:\n      # For grabbable items, the agent needs to be in the 3x3 neighborhood of the item.\n      if temp_state_at_pos.next_to(primitive_idx):\n        can_collect_from_here = True\n        actions_to_complete_collection = [ACTION_USE] # 'USE' action is required for grabbables.\n    elif is_workshop or is_water_stone or is_target_boundary:\n      # For stationary items (workshops, water, stone, or boundary if it's the target),\n      # the agent needs to be directly on the cell.\n      if temp_state_at_pos.grid[cx, cy, primitive_idx] > 0:\n        can_collect_from_here = True\n        actions_to_complete_collection = [] # No 'USE' action needed for these.\n    \n    # If the primitive can be collected from `current_pos`, update the shortest path found.\n    if can_collect_from_here:\n      current_full_path_len = len(actions_to_current_pos) + len(actions_to_complete_collection)\n      \n      # If this path is strictly shorter than the best found so far, update the best.\n      if current_full_path_len < min_total_actions_len:\n        min_total_actions_len = current_full_path_len\n        shortest_path_actions = actions_to_current_pos + actions_to_complete_collection\n      # Note: We do not `break` here. BFS must continue exploring to guarantee the\n      # shortest path to *any* instance of the primitive is found across the entire grid.\n\n    # --- Explore valid neighbors (movement actions) ---\n    for dx, dy, move_action in MOVEMENT_DIRECTIONS:\n      new_x, new_y = cx + dx, cy + dy\n      new_pos = (new_x, new_y)\n\n      # Check 1: Is the new position within grid boundaries?\n      if not (0 <= new_x < WIDTH and 0 <= new_y < HEIGHT):\n        continue # Skip if out of bounds.\n\n      # Check 2: Is the new position blocked by a 'BOUNDARY' unless 'BOUNDARY' is the target itself?\n      # `boundary_kind_idx is not None` check ensures 'BOUNDARY' exists in cookbook, which it usually should.\n      if boundary_kind_idx is not None and grid[new_x, new_y, boundary_kind_idx] > 0:\n        if not is_target_boundary: # If the target is not 'BOUNDARY', and this cell has 'BOUNDARY', it's blocked.\n          continue # This cell is blocked, skip this move.\n        # Else (if is_target_boundary is True), we can move onto it because it's our target.\n\n      # Check 3: Has this position been visited already on a shorter or equal path?\n      if new_pos not in visited:\n          # Pruning 2 (before adding to queue):\n          # Estimate the total path length if we were to reach `new_pos` and\n          # then immediately collect (assuming `new_pos` is itself a collection point, or adjacent for grabbables).\n          # Cost to reach `new_pos` is `len(actions_to_current_pos) + 1`.\n          # Estimated minimal cost from `new_pos` to collect is `1` for grabbable, `0` for others.\n          estimated_total_path_via_new_pos = len(actions_to_current_pos) + 1 + estimated_min_cost_to_collect\n          \n          # Only add `new_pos` to the queue if its estimated total path length\n          # is strictly shorter than the best complete path found so far.\n          if estimated_total_path_via_new_pos < min_total_actions_len: \n              visited.add(new_pos) # Mark `new_pos` as visited.\n              q.append((new_pos, actions_to_current_pos + [move_action])) # Enqueue `new_pos` and its path.\n  \n  # After BFS completes, `shortest_path_actions` will contain the shortest sequence of actions\n  # to collect the primitive, or an empty list if unreachable.\n  return shortest_path_actions", "island_id": 3, "scores": {"3": 0.5}}
{"timestamp": "2025-08-02T10:02:37.994724", "function_name": "collect", "function_body": "  state = env._current_state\n  cookbook = env.world.cookbook\n  grid = state.grid # Access the current grid for primitive locations\n  \n  # 1. Map the primitive name (string) to its integer ID.\n  primitive_idx = cookbook.index[primitive] \n  if primitive_idx is None:\n    # If the primitive name is not recognized, it cannot be collected.\n    return []\n\n  # 2. Early exit: If the primitive is already present in the agent's inventory,\n  # no collection actions are needed from the environment.\n  if state.inventory[primitive_idx] > 0:\n    return []\n\n  # 3. Get action mappings from the environment's action_specs for clarity.\n  action_map = env.action_specs()\n  ACTION_UP = action_map['UP']\n  ACTION_DOWN = action_map['DOWN']\n  ACTION_LEFT = action_map['LEFT']\n  ACTION_RIGHT = action_map['RIGHT']\n  ACTION_USE = action_map['USE']\n\n  # Define possible movement actions as (dx, dy, action_id) tuples.\n  # These define the change in coordinates and the corresponding action index.\n  MOVEMENT_ACTIONS = [\n      (0, -1, ACTION_UP),    # Move North (Up)\n      (0, 1, ACTION_DOWN),   # Move South (Down)\n      (-1, 0, ACTION_LEFT),  # Move West (Left)\n      (1, 0, ACTION_RIGHT),  # Move East (Right)\n  ]\n\n  # Get grid dimensions for boundary checks during pathfinding.\n  WIDTH, HEIGHT, _ = grid.shape\n\n  # Agent's starting position for the Breadth-First Search (BFS).\n  start_pos = state.pos\n\n  # Determine the collection mechanism based on primitive type.\n  # - Grabbable items (e.g., WOOD, IRON, GRASS) require being next to them and using ACTION_USE.\n  # - Stationary items (WORKSHOPs, WATER, STONE, BOUNDARY) require being on their cell.\n  is_grabbable = primitive_idx in env.world.grabbable_indices\n  is_stationary_collectable = (\n      primitive_idx in env.world.workshop_indices or\n      primitive_idx == env.world.water_index or\n      primitive_idx == env.world.stone_index or\n      primitive_idx == cookbook.index['BOUNDARY'] # Explicitly include BOUNDARY here if it's a target\n  )\n  \n  # Identify the index for 'BOUNDARY' for movement blocking logic.\n  boundary_kind_idx = cookbook.index['BOUNDARY']\n  # Flag to indicate if the target primitive itself is 'BOUNDARY'.\n  # This is crucial for movement logic: you can move onto a BOUNDARY if you're trying to collect it.\n  is_target_boundary = (primitive_idx == boundary_kind_idx)\n\n\n  # BFS initialization:\n  # The queue stores tuples: (current_position_tuple, list_of_actions_taken_to_reach_here).\n  q = collections.deque([(start_pos, [])]) \n  # The 'visited' set keeps track of grid positions already explored via a shortest path.\n  visited = {start_pos} \n\n  # Variables to store the shortest path found to a collection point.\n  # `min_total_actions_len` stores the length of the shortest *complete* path (movement + collection action).\n  # `shortest_path_actions` stores the actual sequence of actions for this best path.\n  min_total_actions_len = float('inf') \n  shortest_path_actions = []     \n\n  # BFS traversal loop to find the shortest path to a collectable primitive.\n  while q:\n    current_pos, actions_to_current_pos = q.popleft()\n    cx, cy = current_pos # Unpack current position coordinates.\n\n    # Pruning 1 (after popping from queue):\n    # If the current path length to this position, plus the *minimal* additional cost to collect\n    # (1 for 'USE' if grabbable, 0 otherwise), is already as long as or longer than\n    # the best complete collection path found so far, then this branch cannot lead to a shorter solution.\n    estimated_min_cost_from_current_pos = 1 if is_grabbable else 0\n    if len(actions_to_current_pos) + estimated_min_cost_from_current_pos >= min_total_actions_len:\n        continue # Prune this path and its descendants.\n\n    # Create a hypothetical CraftState at the current BFS position to use helper methods like next_to.\n    # Agent's direction and inventory are kept constant as they don't affect pathfinding geometry,\n    # and we only care about the grid at this potential position.\n    temp_state_at_pos = craft.CraftState(state.scenario, grid, current_pos, state.dir, state.inventory)\n\n    # --- Check if the primitive can be collected from `current_pos` ---\n    can_collect_from_here = False\n    actions_to_complete_collection = [] # Additional actions needed *after* reaching `current_pos` (e.g., USE).\n\n    if is_grabbable:\n      # For grabbable items, the agent needs to be in the 3x3 neighborhood of the item.\n      if temp_state_at_pos.next_to(primitive_idx):\n        can_collect_from_here = True\n        actions_to_complete_collection = [ACTION_USE] # 'USE' action is required for grabbables.\n    elif is_stationary_collectable:\n      # For stationary items (workshops, water, stone, boundary), the agent needs to be directly on the cell.\n      # This check effectively uses the grid directly at the agent's current hypothetical position.\n      if grid[cx, cy, primitive_idx] > 0:\n        can_collect_from_here = True\n        actions_to_complete_collection = [] # No 'USE' action needed for these.\n    \n    # If the primitive can be collected from `current_pos`, update the shortest path found.\n    if can_collect_from_here:\n      current_full_path_len = len(actions_to_current_pos) + len(actions_to_complete_collection)\n      \n      # If this path is strictly shorter than the best found so far, update the best.\n      if current_full_path_len < min_total_actions_len:\n        min_total_actions_len = current_full_path_len\n        shortest_path_actions = actions_to_current_pos + actions_to_complete_collection\n      # Note: We do not `break` here. BFS must continue exploring to guarantee the\n      # shortest path to *any* instance of the primitive is found.\n\n    # --- Explore valid neighbors (movement actions) ---\n    for dx, dy, move_action in MOVEMENT_ACTIONS:\n      new_x, new_y = cx + dx, cy + dy\n      new_pos = (new_x, new_y)\n\n      # Check if the new position is within grid boundaries.\n      if 0 <= new_x < WIDTH and 0 <= new_y < HEIGHT:\n        # Check for impassable terrain (e.g., BOUNDARY).\n        # A cell is impassable if it contains a BOUNDARY AND the target primitive is NOT BOUNDARY.\n        if boundary_kind_idx is not None and grid[new_x, new_y, boundary_kind_idx] > 0:\n          if not is_target_boundary: # Only block movement if the boundary is just an obstacle, not the target itself.\n            continue # This cell is blocked, skip this move.\n          # Else (if is_target_boundary is True), we can move onto it because it's our target.\n\n        # Check if this position has not been visited yet through a shorter or equal path.\n        if new_pos not in visited:\n          # Pruning 2 (before adding to queue):\n          # Estimate the total path length if we were to reach `new_pos` and\n          # then immediately collect (assuming `new_pos` is itself a collection point, or adjacent for grabbables).\n          # This acts as an admissible heuristic for an A*-like pruning, where edge costs are 1.\n          # Cost to reach `new_pos` is `len(actions_to_current_pos) + 1`.\n          # Estimated minimal cost from `new_pos` to collect is `1` for grabbable, `0` for others.\n          estimated_total_path_via_new_pos = len(actions_to_current_pos) + 1 + (1 if is_grabbable else 0)\n          \n          # Only add `new_pos` to the queue if its estimated total path length\n          # is strictly shorter than the best complete path found so far.\n          if estimated_total_path_via_new_pos < min_total_actions_len: \n              visited.add(new_pos) # Mark `new_pos` as visited.\n              q.append((new_pos, actions_to_current_pos + [move_action])) # Enqueue `new_pos` and its path.\n  \n  # After BFS completes, `shortest_path_actions` will contain the shortest sequence of actions\n  # to collect the primitive, or an empty list if unreachable.\n  return shortest_path_actions", "island_id": 3, "scores": {"3": 0.5}}
{"timestamp": "2025-08-02T10:03:39.601894", "function_name": "collect", "function_body": "  \"\"\"Returns a list of actions to find and collect the primitive in the passed env.\n    This version refines the BFS logic for clarity and robustness, building on collect_v1.\n    \n    Args:\n        env (env.CraftLab): The CraftLab object.\n        primitive (str): The name of the primitive to collect.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[int]: A sequence of action indices to execute.\n  \"\"\"\n  current_state = env._current_state\n  cookbook = env.world.cookbook\n  grid = current_state.grid # The current state's grid for querying primitive locations.\n  \n  # 1. Map the primitive name (string) to its integer ID.\n  primitive_idx = cookbook.index[primitive] \n  if primitive_idx is None:\n    # If the primitive name is not recognized in the cookbook, it cannot be collected.\n    return []\n\n  # 2. Early exit: If the primitive is already present in the agent's inventory,\n  # no collection actions are needed from the environment.\n  if current_state.inventory[primitive_idx] > 0:\n    return []\n\n  # 3. Get action mappings for clarity in action sequences.\n  action_map = env.action_specs()\n  ACTION_UP = action_map['UP']\n  ACTION_DOWN = action_map['DOWN']\n  ACTION_LEFT = action_map['LEFT']\n  ACTION_RIGHT = action_map['RIGHT']\n  ACTION_USE = action_map['USE']\n\n  # Define possible movement actions as (dx, dy, action_id) tuples.\n  # These tuples represent (change in X, change in Y, corresponding action index).\n  MOVEMENT_ACTIONS = [\n      (0, -1, ACTION_UP),    # Move North (decreases Y)\n      (0, 1, ACTION_DOWN),   # Move South (increases Y)\n      (-1, 0, ACTION_LEFT),  # Move West (decreases X)\n      (1, 0, ACTION_RIGHT),  # Move East (increases X)\n  ]\n\n  # Get grid dimensions for boundary checks.\n  WIDTH, HEIGHT, _ = grid.shape\n\n  # Agent's starting position for the BFS.\n  start_pos = current_state.pos\n\n  # Determine the collection mechanism based on primitive type.\n  # - Grabbable items (e.g., WOOD, IRON, GRASS) require being next to them and using ACTION_USE.\n  # - Stationary items (WORKSHOPs, WATER, STONE, BOUNDARY) require being on their cell and don't need ACTION_USE.\n  is_grabbable = primitive_idx in env.world.grabbable_indices\n  \n  # A primitive is \"stationary\" if it's not grabbable and is one of the specific types that require being on the cell.\n  # Note: 'BOUNDARY' can also be a stationary target if we are trying to collect it.\n  is_stationary_target = (\n      primitive_idx in env.world.workshop_indices or\n      primitive_idx == env.world.water_index or\n      primitive_idx == env.world.stone_index or\n      primitive_idx == cookbook.index['BOUNDARY'] # Check if the target itself is 'BOUNDARY'\n  )\n\n  # Get the index for the 'BOUNDARY' kind. Used to determine impassable terrain.\n  # This check assumes 'BOUNDARY' always exists in the cookbook, which is typical for such environments.\n  boundary_kind_idx = cookbook.index['BOUNDARY'] \n  # Check if the *target primitive itself* is 'BOUNDARY'. This affects movement rules.\n  is_target_boundary = (primitive_idx == boundary_kind_idx)\n\n  # BFS initialization:\n  # Queue stores tuples: (current_position_tuple, list_of_actions_taken_to_reach_here).\n  q = collections.deque([(start_pos, [])]) \n  # Visited set keeps track of grid positions already explored to avoid cycles and redundant paths.\n  visited = {start_pos} \n\n  # Variables to store the shortest path found.\n  min_total_actions_len = float('inf') # Stores the length of the shortest complete path (movement + collection action).\n  shortest_path_actions = []            # Stores the actual sequence of actions for this best path.\n\n  # Calculate the minimum additional cost (actions) required *after* reaching a collection point.\n  # This is 1 for grabbable items (for the 'USE' action), and 0 for stationary items.\n  min_collection_cost_at_point = 1 if is_grabbable else 0\n\n  # BFS traversal loop to find the shortest path.\n  while q:\n    current_pos, actions_to_current_pos = q.popleft()\n    cx, cy = current_pos \n\n    # Pruning 1 (after popping from queue):\n    # If the current path length (to this position) plus the minimum cost to collect\n    # (if applicable from this position) is already equal to or longer than the\n    # best complete path found so far, then this branch cannot lead to a shorter solution.\n    if len(actions_to_current_pos) + min_collection_cost_at_point >= min_total_actions_len:\n        continue # Prune this path.\n\n    # --- Check if the primitive can be collected from `current_pos` ---\n    can_collect_from_here = False\n    actions_for_collection = [] # Actions needed *after* reaching `current_pos` (e.g., ACTION_USE).\n\n    if is_grabbable:\n      # For grabbable items, the agent needs to be in the 3x3 neighborhood of the item.\n      # Manually check the 3x3 area around `current_pos`.\n      found_in_neighborhood = False\n      for dx_adj in [-1, 0, 1]:\n        for dy_adj in [-1, 0, 1]:\n          adj_x, adj_y = cx + dx_adj, cy + dy_adj\n          # Ensure adjacent cell is within grid boundaries.\n          if 0 <= adj_x < WIDTH and 0 <= adj_y < HEIGHT:\n            # Check if the primitive is present in the adjacent cell.\n            if grid[adj_x, adj_y, primitive_idx] > 0:\n              found_in_neighborhood = True\n              break \n        if found_in_neighborhood:\n            break \n      \n      if found_in_neighborhood:\n        can_collect_from_here = True\n        actions_for_collection = [ACTION_USE] # 'USE' action is required.\n    elif is_stationary_target:\n      # For stationary items, the agent needs to be directly on the cell.\n      if grid[cx, cy, primitive_idx] > 0:\n        can_collect_from_here = True\n        actions_for_collection = [] # No 'USE' action needed.\n    \n    # If the primitive can be collected from `current_pos`, update the shortest path found.\n    if can_collect_from_here:\n      current_full_path_len = len(actions_to_current_pos) + len(actions_for_collection)\n      \n      # If this path is strictly shorter, update the best.\n      if current_full_path_len < min_total_actions_len:\n        min_total_actions_len = current_full_path_len\n        shortest_path_actions = actions_to_current_pos + actions_for_collection\n      # BFS continues to explore in case an equally short path leads to another instance\n      # or a different path to the same instance. This ensures correctness for general BFS.\n\n    # --- Explore valid neighbors (movement actions) ---\n    for dx, dy, move_action in MOVEMENT_ACTIONS:\n      new_x, new_y = cx + dx, cy + dy\n      new_pos = (new_x, new_y)\n\n      # Check 1: Is the new position within grid boundaries?\n      if not (0 <= new_x < WIDTH and 0 <= new_y < HEIGHT):\n        continue \n\n      # Check 2: Is the new position blocked by 'BOUNDARY'?\n      # A cell is blocked if it contains 'BOUNDARY' AND the target primitive is NOT 'BOUNDARY'.\n      # If the target *is* boundary, we are allowed to move onto it.\n      if grid[new_x, new_y, boundary_kind_idx] > 0 and not is_target_boundary:\n          continue # Blocked, skip this move.\n\n      # Check 3: Has this position been visited already on a shorter or equal path?\n      if new_pos not in visited:\n          # Pruning 2 (before adding to queue):\n          # Estimate the total path length if we were to reach `new_pos` and\n          # then immediately collect from there (using the minimum collection cost).\n          estimated_path_len_through_new_pos = len(actions_to_current_pos) + 1 + min_collection_cost_at_point\n          \n          # Only add `new_pos` to the queue if its estimated total path length\n          # is strictly shorter than the best complete path found so far.\n          if estimated_path_len_through_new_pos < min_total_actions_len: \n              visited.add(new_pos) \n              q.append((new_pos, actions_to_current_pos + [move_action])) \n  \n  # After BFS completes, `shortest_path_actions` will contain the shortest sequence of actions,\n  # or an empty list if the primitive is unreachable.\n  return shortest_path_actions", "island_id": 3, "scores": {"3": -1}}
